    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s.\nConsider performing the following operation until s becomes empty:\n\nFor every alphabet character from 'a' to 'z', remove the first occurrence of that character in s (if it exists).\n\nFor example, let initially s = \"aabcbbca\". We do the following operations:\n\nRemove the underlined characters s = \"aabcbbca\". The resulting string is s = \"abbca\".\nRemove the underlined characters s = \"abbca\". The resulting string is s = \"ba\".\nRemove the underlined characters s = \"ba\". The resulting string is s = \"\".\n\nReturn the value of the string s right before applying the last operation. In the example above, answer is \"ba\".\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"aabcbbca\"\nOutput: \"ba\"\nExplanation: Explained in the statement.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"abcd\"\nOutput: \"abcd\"\nExplanation: We do the following operation:\n- Remove the underlined characters s = \"abcd\". The resulting string is s = \"\".\nThe string just before the last operation is \"abcd\".\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^5\ns consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef lastNonEmptyString(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    To find the string right before the last operation, we need to identify\n    which characters persist the longest. This corresponds to characters \n    that have the maximum frequency in the original string.\n    \n    The last operation will remove one occurrence of every character currently present.\n    Therefore, the characters remaining just before the last operation are those\n    that appear for the 'max_frequency'-th time in the string.\n    \"\"\"\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    # Count the frequency of each character\n    counts = Counter(s)\n    max_freq = max(counts.values())\n    \n    # We only care about characters that appear max_freq times\n    # and we want to keep only their very last occurrence.\n    # To maintain the original relative order, we iterate backwards or\n    # track how many times we've seen each character.\n    \n    result_chars = []\n    current_counts = {}\n    \n    # Iterate through the string and track the occurrence count of each char.\n    # If a character's current occurrence count equals max_freq, \n    # it means this specific instance belongs to the string before the last deletion.\n    for char in s:\n        current_counts[char] = current_counts.get(char, 0) + 1\n        if current_counts[char] == max_freq:\n            result_chars.append(char)\n            \n    return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```",
