    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_347",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nA regular pentagon P is shown in the figure below.\n\nDetermine whether the length of the line segment connecting points S_1 and S_2 of P equals the length of the line segment connecting points T_1 and T_2.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nS_1S_2\r\nT_1T_2\n\nOutput\n\nIf the length of the line segment connecting points S_1 and S_2 of P equals the length of the line segment connecting points T_1 and T_2, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- Each of S_1, S_2, T_1, and T_2 is one of the characters A, B, C, D, and E.\n- S_1 \\neq S_2\n- T_1 \\neq T_2\n\nSample Input 1\n\nAC\r\nEC\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\n\nThe length of the line segment connecting point A and point C of P equals the length of the line segment connecting point E and point C.\n\nSample Input 2\n\nDA\r\nEA\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\r\n\nThe length of the line segment connecting point D and point A of P does not equal the length of the line segment connecting point E and point A.\n\nSample Input 3\n\nBD\r\nBD\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we represent the vertices of a regular pentagon (A, B, C, D, E) as integers (0, 1, 2, 3, 4). In a regular pentagon, there are only two possible distances between any two distinct vertices:\n1.  **Adjacent sides** (e.g., A-B, B-C, etc.): The distance between their indices (modulo 5) is 1 or 4.\n2.  **Diagonals** (e.g., A-C, A-D, etc.): The distance between their indices (modulo 5) is 2 or 3.\n\nBy calculating the absolute difference between the positions of the two given points and considering the circular nature of the pentagon (distance $d$ is the same as $5-d$), we can determine if two segments have the same length.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read the two lines of input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    s_segment = input_data[0]\n    t_segment = input_data[1]\n    \n    # Map the vertices A-E to indices 0-4\n    mapping = {'A': 0, 'B': 1, 'C': 2, 'D': 3, 'E': 4}\n    \n    def get_length_type(seg):\n        # Calculate the circular distance between the two vertices\n        p1 = mapping[seg[0]]\n        p2 = mapping[seg[1]]\n        diff = abs(p1 - p2)\n        # In a pentagon, distance 1 is the same as 4 (side)\n        # Distance 2 is the same as 3 (diagonal)\n        return min(diff, 5 - diff)\n\n    # If the minimal circular distances are equal, the lengths are equal\n    if get_length_type(s_segment) == get_length_type(t_segment):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
