    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfRightTriangles(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    To form a right triangle where one point (the vertex) is at grid[r][c], \n    we need to pick one other '1' from the same row and one other '1' \n    from the same column.\n    \n    If row r has 'rowCount' ones and column c has 'colCount' ones, \n    the number of right triangles with the vertex at (r, c) is:\n    (rowCount - 1) * (colCount - 1)\n    \n    We sum this value for every cell (r, c) where grid[r][c] == 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Precompute the number of 1s in each row\n    row_counts = [sum(row) for row in grid]\n    \n    # Precompute the number of 1s in each column\n    col_counts = [0] * cols\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                col_counts[c] += 1\n                \n    total_triangles = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        # Optimization: if a row has less than 2 ones, it can't be part of a triangle base\n        if row_counts[r] < 2:\n            continue\n        for c in range(cols):\n            # Check if current cell can be the vertex of a right triangle\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                # Potential partners in the same row: (row_counts[r] - 1)\n                # Potential partners in the same column: (col_counts[c] - 1)\n                total_triangles += (row_counts[r] - 1) * (col_counts[c] - 1)\n                \n    return total_triangles\n```",
