    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef sumImbalanceNumbers(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the sum of imbalance numbers of all subarrays of the input array.\n    The time complexity is O(n^2) due to the constraints (n <= 1000).\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_imbalance = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # Using a set to keep track of seen elements in the current subarray\n        # and a variable to maintain the current imbalance count.\n        seen = set()\n        seen.add(nums[i])\n        current_imbalance = 0\n        \n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            val = nums[j]\n            if val not in seen:\n                # If the value is new to the subarray, check if it fills or creates gaps.\n                # A new value creates an imbalance unless it is adjacent to existing values.\n                # If neither (val-1) nor (val+1) is present, imbalance increases.\n                # If both (val-1) and (val+1) are present, the gap between them is bridged, decreasing imbalance.\n                \n                increment = 1\n                if (val - 1) in seen:\n                    increment -= 1\n                if (val + 1) in seen:\n                    increment -= 1\n                \n                current_imbalance += increment\n                seen.add(val)\n            \n            total_imbalance += current_imbalance\n            \n    return total_imbalance\n```",
