      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input data\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    # Parse N, D, P\n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    d = int(input_data[1])\n    p = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    # Parse fares F_i\n    f = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        f.append(int(input_data[3 + i]))\n    \n    # Sort fares in descending order to prioritize using passes on expensive days\n    f.sort(reverse=True)\n    \n    # Calculate prefix sums to quickly get the sum of fares being replaced\n    # sum_f[i] will store the sum of the i most expensive days\n    sum_f = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        sum_f[i + 1] = sum_f[i] + f[i]\n    \n    total_fare_all_days = sum_f[n]\n    min_cost = total_fare_all_days\n    \n    # Try buying k batches of D passes\n    # We only need to check k up to ceil(N/D)\n    # k=0 means no passes, k=(n+d-1)//d means covering potentially all days\n    for k in range(1, (n + d - 1) // d + 1):\n        num_covered = min(n, k * d)\n        # Cost = cost of k batches + sum of remaining fares\n        current_cost = k * p + (total_fare_all_days - sum_f[num_covered])\n        if current_cost < min_cost:\n            min_cost = current_cost\n        else:\n            # Since f is sorted descending, if adding more passes doesn't help\n            # it's unlikely to help further (cost function is convex-ish)\n            # However, for safety and N=2*10^5, we can check all k.\n            pass\n            \n    print(min_cost)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
