      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read the input string and remove any trailing whitespace\n    s = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    if not s:\n        return\n    \n    n = len(s)\n    mod = 998244353\n\n    # DP state: dp[i][j]\n    # i: 0 -> no matching 'X' (the first character of \"XXyX\")\n    # i: 1 -> found the first 'X'\n    # i: 2 -> found 'XX'\n    # i: 3 -> found 'XXy'\n    # Each character can be Uppercase (U) or Lowercase (L).\n    # There are 26 U and 26 L.\n    \n    # dp[0]: No 'X' yet\n    # dp[1]: Found one 'X'\n    # dp[2]: Found 'XX' (consecutive or not, as subsequence)\n    # dp[3]: Found 'XXy' (two same uppercase and one lowercase)\n    \n    dp = [0] * 4\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    for char in s:\n        new_dp = [0] * 4\n        \n        # Calculate number of choices for Uppercase and Lowercase\n        if char == '?':\n            u_choices = 26\n            l_choices = 26\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            u_choices = 1\n            l_choices = 0\n        else:\n            u_choices = 0\n            l_choices = 1\n            \n        # If we choose an Uppercase letter:\n        if u_choices > 0:\n            # dp[0] -> dp[1]: Found first 'X'. (All 26 options possible)\n            # However, this DP needs to account for the fact that 'XX' means\n            # specifically two of the *same* uppercase letters.\n            # This requires tracking which letter we picked. \n            # But the constraint is \"do not contain as a subsequence\".\n            # This is simpler:\n            # Let's redefine state:\n            # count0: strings with no Uppercase letters\n            # count1: total strings (standard transition)\n            pass\n\n    # Correct approach for the specific \"XXyX\" subsequence:\n    # A string contains XXyX if there exist indices i < j < k < l \n    # s[i]==s[j] are Upper, s[k] is Lower, s[l] is Upper.\n    \n    # Since any XXy followed by ANY uppercase letter forms the subsequence,\n    # and the condition is s[i]==s[j], we track:\n    # dp[0]: count of ways to have no 'X'\n    # dp[1][char]: count of ways to have one 'char' (U)\n    # dp[2]: count of ways to have 'XX' (two same U)\n    # dp[3]: count of ways to have 'XXy' (two same U then one L)\n    \n    # State:\n    # c0: sum of ways to have 0 Uppercase letters\n    # c1: sum of ways to have 1 Uppercase letter (specifically, sum over all 26)\n    # c2: sum of ways to have 'XX'\n    # c3: sum of ways to have 'XXy'\n    \n    c0 = 1\n    c1 = 0\n    c2 = 0\n    c3 = 0\n    \n    for char in s:\n        nc0, nc1, nc2, nc3 = 0, 0, 0, 0\n        \n        # Choice: Lowercase letter\n        l_ways = 26 if char == '?' else (1 if 'a' <= char <= 'z' else 0)\n        if l_ways > 0:\n            nc0 = (nc0 + c0 * l_ways) % mod\n            nc1 = (nc1 + c1 * l_ways) % mod\n            nc2 = (nc2 + c2 * l_ways) % mod\n            nc3 = (nc3 + (c3 + c2) * l_ways) % mod\n            \n        # Choice: Uppercase letter\n        u_ways = 26 if char == '?' else (1 if 'A' <= char <= 'Z' else 0)\n        if u_ways > 0:\n            if char == '?':\n                # For each of the 26 U-letters:\n                # 1. c0 -> becomes the 1st 'X' for this specific letter\n                # 2. c1 (which is sum of all single U) -> the 'current' U matches one existing U, \n                #    or is a new first U.\n                # Actually, if we pick 'A', it matches the existing 'A' in c1.\n                # There is only 1 'A' in the existing pool of single letters.\n                \n                # new c1: c0 * 26 + c1\n                nc1 = (nc1 + c0 * 26 + c1) % mod\n                # new c2: c2 + (c1/26 * 1 * 26) -> every existing single U finds a match\n                nc2 = (nc2 + c2 * 26 + c1) % mod\n                # new c3: c3 * 26 (XXy is followed by U, but that would complete the sequence, \n                # so we only stay in c3 if we DON'T complete it. \n                # But the question asks for those that DO NOT contain it.\n                # So if we have XXy and add any U, it's a fail.\n                nc3 = (nc3 + c3 * 26) % mod\n            else:\n                # Specific uppercase letter 'char'\n                # All c0 become c1 for this specific letter.\n                nc1 = (nc1 + c0 + c1) % mod\n                # If there was already a 'char' in c1, it becomes c2. \n                # We need to track individual U counts to be precise.\n                # Let's rethink.\n                pass\n\n    # The logic above is slightly flawed because c1 needs to track each letter.\n    # Let's simplify:\n    # dp0: ways to have 0 Uppercase\n    # dp1: ways to have 1 Uppercase (summed)\n    # dp2: ways to have 'XX' (summed)\n    # dp3: ways to have 'XXy' (summed)\n    \n    # At each step:\n    # If Lower: dp0*=26, dp1*=26, dp2*=26, dp3 = dp3*26 + dp2*26\n    # If Upper(?): dp3*=26 (can't add U to XXy), dp2 = dp2*26 + dp1*1, dp1 = dp1*1 + dp0*26\n    \n    # Let's trace ? (26 options): \n    # dp1: each of 26 letters can be started (dp0*26). Existing dp1 letters stay dp1.\n    # dp2: each of 26 letters in dp1 can be completed (dp1*1). Existing dp2 stay dp2.\n    \n    d0, d1, d2, d3 = 1, 0, 0, 0\n    for char in s:\n        if char == '?':\n            nd0 = (d0 * 26) % mod\n            nd1 = (d1 + d0 * 26) % mod\n            nd2 = (d2 * 26 + d1) % mod\n            nd3 = (d3 * 26 + d2 * 26) % mod\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':\n            nd0 = 0\n            nd1 = (d0 + d1) % mod\n            nd2 = (d2 + d1) % mod\n            nd3 = (d3) % mod # Any U added to d3 completes XXyX, so we discard it\n        else: # Lowercase\n            nd0 = d0\n            nd1 = d1\n            nd2 = d2\n            nd3 = (d3 + d2) % mod\n        d0, d1, d2, d3 = nd0, nd1, nd2, nd3\n\n    print((d0 + d1 + d2 + d3) % mod)\n\nsolve()\n```",
