    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_494",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s, which is known to be a concatenation of anagrams of some string t.\nReturn the minimum possible length of the string t.\nAn anagram is formed by rearranging the letters of a string. For example, \"aab\", \"aba\", and, \"baa\" are anagrams of \"aab\".\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"abba\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation:\nOne possible string t could be \"ba\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"cdef\"\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nOne possible string t could be \"cdef\", notice that t can be equal to s.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport collections\n\ndef minAnagramLength(s: str) -> int:\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Pre-calculate frequency counts for the entire string\n    total_counts = collections.Counter(s)\n    \n    # The length of t must be a divisor of n\n    # We iterate through possible lengths L from 1 upwards\n    for L in range(1, n + 1):\n        if n % L == 0:\n            # If s is a concatenation of anagrams of t, \n            # then every segment of length L must have the same letter frequencies.\n            \n            # First, check if the total counts are divisible by (n/L)\n            # If they aren't, this L cannot be the length of t.\n            num_segments = n // L\n            possible = True\n            for char in total_counts:\n                if total_counts[char] % num_segments != 0:\n                    possible = False\n                    break\n            \n            if not possible:\n                continue\n            \n            # Next, check if every L-length segment has the same frequency profile\n            # We only need to check if the first segment's profile matches the expected profile\n            target_counts = collections.Counter(s[:L])\n            \n            # To be sure, verify all segments\n            # In competitive programming, comparing against the first segment is usually enough\n            # because the total_counts check already filters out most cases.\n            is_valid = True\n            for i in range(L, n, L):\n                segment_counts = collections.Counter(s[i:i+L])\n                if segment_counts != target_counts:\n                    is_valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if is_valid:\n                return L\n    return n\n```",
