    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport math\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\ndef findKthSmallest(coins, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    To find the k-th smallest amount, we can use binary search on the answer range.\n    The range is [min(coins), min(coins) * k].\n    For a value x, we need to count how many multiples of the given coins exist <= x.\n    Since we cannot combine different denominations (just their multiples), \n    we use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion (PIE) to count the size of the union \n    of sets of multiples for each coin.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Precompute LCMs for all combinations to speed up the counting function\n    n = len(coins)\n    lcm_groups = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        group = []\n        for combo in combinations(coins, i):\n            res = combo[0]\n            for val in combo[1:]:\n                res = (res * val) // math.gcd(res, val)\n            group.append(res)\n        lcm_groups.append(group)\n\n    def count_multiples(x):\n        total = 0\n        for i, lcms in enumerate(lcm_groups):\n            # Inclusion-Exclusion: add for odd number of sets, subtract for even\n            term_sum = sum(x // val for val in lcms)\n            if i % 2 == 0:\n                total += term_sum\n            else:\n                total -= term_sum\n        return total\n\n    low = k\n    high = min(coins) * k\n    ans = high\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if count_multiples(mid) >= k:\n            ans = mid\n            high = mid - 1\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n            \n    return ans\n```",
