    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of integers nums of length n, and a positive odd integer k.\nThe strength of x subarrays is defined as strength = sum[1] * x - sum[2] * (x - 1) + sum[3] * (x - 2) - sum[4] * (x - 3) + ... + sum[x] * 1 where sum[i] is the sum of the elements in the i^th subarray. Formally, strength is sum of (-1)^i+1 * sum[i] * (x - i + 1) over all i's such that 1 <= i <= x.\nYou need to select k disjoint subarrays from nums, such that their strength is maximum.\nReturn the maximum possible strength that can be obtained.\nNote that the selected subarrays don't need to cover the entire array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,-1,2], k = 3\nOutput: 22\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 3 subarrays is: nums[0..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is (1 + 2 + 3) * 3 - (-1) * 2 + 2 * 1 = 22.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [12,-2,-2,-2,-2], k = 5\nOutput: 64\nExplanation: The only possible way to select 5 disjoint subarrays is: nums[0..0], nums[1..1], nums[2..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is 12 * 5 - (-2) * 4 + (-2) * 3 - (-2) * 2 + (-2) * 1 = 64.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [-1,-2,-3], k = 1\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 1 subarray is: nums[0..0]. The strength is -1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n <= 10^4\n-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= k <= n\n1 <= n * k <= 10^6\nk is odd.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumStrength(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum strength by selecting k disjoint subarrays.\n    The strength is defined as sum_{i=1}^k (-1)^(i+1) * sum[i] * (k - i + 1).\n    We use dynamic programming with space optimization.\n    \n    dp[i][j] represents the maximum strength using j subarrays from the first i elements.\n    To optimize space, we use two rows: prev_dp and curr_dp.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # prev_dp[j] is the max strength using j subarrays considering elements up to i-1\n    # We use -float('inf') to represent unreachable states\n    prev_dp = [0] + [-float('inf')] * k\n    \n    # We need to track the best possible previous state to start or continue a subarray\n    # best_prev[j] will store max(prev_dp[j-1] - current_val_at_start, \n    #                            continuing_current_subarray)\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        curr_dp = [0] + [-float('inf')] * k\n        for j in range(1, k + 1):\n            # The weight for the j-th subarray\n            weight = k - j + 1\n            if j % 2 == 0:\n                weight = -weight\n            \n            # Option 1: Don't include nums[i-1] in any new or existing j-th subarray\n            # This means the max strength is the same as the max strength using j subarrays from i-1 elements\n            res = prev_dp[j]\n            \n            # Option 2: Include nums[i-1] in the j-th subarray\n            # This is handled more efficiently by maintaining the max possible value \n            # including the current element in the j-th subarray across iterations.\n            # However, for a clean single-function implementation, we can use a helper array.\n            \n        # Due to n*k <= 10^6, a 2D DP or optimized 1D DP is required.\n        # Let's redefine the state to handle the \"current subarray\" status.\n        pass\n\n    # Optimized DP:\n    # dp[j] -> max strength using j subarrays ending at or before current index\n    # hold[j] -> max strength using j subarrays, with the j-th subarray ending exactly at current index\n    dp = [0] * (k + 1)\n    hold = [-float('inf')] * (k + 1)\n    \n    for x in nums:\n        for j in range(k, 0, -1):\n            weight = (k - j + 1) if j % 2 == 1 else -(k - j + 1)\n            \n            # hold[j] can be:\n            # 1. Starting a new (j-th) subarray from the best dp[j-1]\n            # 2. Continuing the existing j-th subarray\n            hold[j] = max(dp[j-1] + x * weight, hold[j] + x * weight)\n            \n            # dp[j] can be:\n            # 1. Not picking current element for j-th subarray (stay with previous dp[j])\n            # 2. Ending the j-th subarray at the current element\n            dp[j] = max(dp[j], hold[j])\n            \n    return dp[k]\n```",
