      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from standard input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    # First element is N\n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    points = []\n    \n    # Parse the coordinates for N points\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = int(input_data[1 + 2*i])\n        y = int(input_data[2 + 2*i])\n        points.append((x, y))\n        \n    # Iterate through each point to find its farthest neighbor\n    for i in range(n):\n        max_dist_sq = -1\n        farthest_id = -1\n        \n        x1, y1 = points[i]\n        \n        for j in range(n):\n            if i == j:\n                continue\n            \n            x2, y2 = points[j]\n            # Calculate squared Euclidean distance to avoid square root (sufficient for comparison)\n            dist_sq = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2\n            \n            # Check if this point is farther than the current maximum\n            if dist_sq > max_dist_sq:\n                max_dist_sq = dist_sq\n                farthest_id = j + 1 # Points are 1-indexed\n            elif dist_sq == max_dist_sq:\n                # If distances are equal, pick the one with the smaller ID\n                # Since we iterate j from 0 to n-1, the first one encountered is already the smallest\n                pass\n                \n        print(farthest_id)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
