    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef checkArray(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    To make all elements zero using subarrays of size k, we must process the array \n    from left to right greedily. For each element nums[i], if it is greater than zero,\n    we must start a \"reduction\" of value nums[i] that covers the range [i, i + k - 1].\n    \n    We use a difference array approach (or a sliding window sum) to keep track of \n    how much has been subtracted from the current element based on operations \n    started at previous indices.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # current_diff keeps track of the total reduction applied to the current index i\n    current_diff = 0\n    # diff_array stores the points where a reduction window ends\n    diff_array = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # Update current_diff by adding the \"end of window\" value\n        current_diff += diff_array[i]\n        \n        # Calculate the actual value of nums[i] after previous operations\n        val = nums[i] - current_diff\n        \n        if val < 0:\n            # We have already subtracted more than the element's value\n            return False\n        \n        if val > 0:\n            # If we need to reduce this element to 0, we must start a window here.\n            # A window starting at i must be of size k, so it ends at i + k - 1.\n            if i + k > n:\n                # Not enough space to fit a subarray of size k\n                return False\n            \n            # Apply the reduction\n            current_diff += val\n            # Mark the end of this reduction window at index i + k\n            if i + k <= n:\n                diff_array[i + k] -= val\n                \n    return True\n```",
