    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid with H rows and W columns. Initially, all cells are painted with color 0.\nYou will perform the following operations in the order i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M.\n\n- \nIf T_i = 1, repaint all cells in the A_i-th row with color X_i.\n\n- \nIf T_i = 2, repaint all cells in the A_i-th column with color X_i.\n\n\nAfter all operations are completed, for each color i that exists on the grid, find the number of cells that are painted with color i.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W M\nT_1 A_1 X_1\nT_2 A_2 X_2\n\\vdots\nT_M A_M X_M\n\nOutput\n\nLet K be the number of distinct integers i such that there are cells painted with color i. Print K + 1 lines.\nThe first line should contain the value of K.\nThe second and subsequent lines should contain, for each color i that exists on the grid, the color number i and the number of cells painted with that color.\nSpecifically, the (i + 1)-th line (1 \\leq i \\leq K) should contain the color number c_i and the number of cells x_i painted with color c_i, in this order, separated by a space.\nHere, print the color numbers in ascending order. That is, ensure that c_1 < c_2 < \\ldots < c_K. Note also that x_i > 0 is required.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq H, W, M \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- T_i \\in \\lbrace 1, 2 \\rbrace\n- 1 \\leq A_i \\leq H for each i such that T_i = 1,\n- 1 \\leq A_i \\leq W for each i such that T_i = 2.\n- 0 \\leq X_i \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 4 4\n1 2 5\n2 4 0\n1 3 3\n1 3 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\n0 5\n2 4\n5 3\n\nThe operations will change the colors of the cells in the grid as follows:\n0000   0000   0000   0000   0000\n0000 → 5555 → 5550 → 5550 → 5550 \n0000   0000   0000   3333   2222\n\nEventually, there are five cells painted with color 0, four with color 2, and three with color 5.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 1 5\n1 1 1\n1 1 10\n2 1 100\n1 1 1000\n2 1 10000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n10000 1\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5 5 10\n1 1 1\n1 2 2\n1 3 3\n1 4 4\n1 5 5\n2 1 6\n2 2 7\n2 3 8\n2 4 9\n2 5 10\n\nSample Output 3\n\n5\n6 5\n7 5\n8 5\n9 5\n10 5",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Use fast I/O\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    H = int(input_data[0])\n    W = int(input_data[1])\n    M = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    queries = []\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(M):\n        t = int(input_data[idx])\n        a = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        x = int(input_data[idx+2])\n        queries.append((t, a, x))\n        idx += 3\n        \n    # To determine the final color of a cell, we look at operations in reverse.\n    # The last operation applied to a specific row or column is the one that sticks.\n    rows_filled = set()\n    cols_filled = set()\n    \n    color_counts = {}\n    \n    # Process queries from last to first\n    for i in range(M - 1, -1, -1):\n        t, a, x = queries[i]\n        \n        if t == 1: # Row operation\n            if a not in rows_filled:\n                # This row is being painted. Only cells in columns not yet \n                # processed in the reverse order will actually be colored by this.\n                count = W - len(cols_filled)\n                if count > 0:\n                    color_counts[x] = color_counts.get(x, 0) + count\n                rows_filled.add(a)\n        else: # Column operation\n            if a not in cols_filled:\n                # This column is being painted. Only cells in rows not yet\n                # processed will be colored by this.\n                count = H - len(rows_filled)\n                if count > 0:\n                    color_counts[x] = color_counts.get(x, 0) + count\n                cols_filled.add(a)\n                \n    # Cells that were never painted by any operation remain color 0\n    remaining_cells = (H * W) - sum(color_counts.values())\n    if remaining_cells > 0:\n        color_counts[0] = color_counts.get(0, 0) + remaining_cells\n        \n    # Get sorted results\n    result_colors = sorted([c for c, count in color_counts.items() if count > 0])\n    \n    print(len(result_colors))\n    for c in result_colors:\n        print(f\"{c} {color_counts[c]}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n\n```",
