    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given two strings: S, which consists of uppercase English letters and has length N, and T, which also consists of uppercase English letters and has length M\\ (\\leq N).\nThere is a string X of length N consisting only of the character #. Determine whether it is possible to make X match S by performing the following operation any number of times:\n\n- Choose M consecutive characters in X and replace them with T.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\nT\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Yes if it is possible to make X match S; print No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq \\min(N, 5)\n- S is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length N.\n- T is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length M.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n7 3\nABCBABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nBelow, let X[l:r] denote the part from the l-th through the r-th character of X.\nYou can make X match S by operating as follows.\n\n- Replace X[3:5] with T. X becomes ##ABC##.\n- Replace X[1:3] with T. X becomes ABCBC##.\n- Replace X[5:7] with T. X becomes ABCBABC.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 3\nABBCABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nNo matter how you operate, it is impossible to make X match S.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n12 2\nXYXXYXXYYYXY\nXY\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(input_data[0])\n    m = int(input_data[1])\n    s = input_data[2]\n    t = input_data[3]\n    \n    # visited[i] tracks if we have processed the substring starting at index i\n    # covered[i] tracks if the character at index i has been matched\n    visited = [False] * (n - m + 1)\n    covered = [False] * n\n    queue = deque()\n    \n    def can_place(i):\n        \"\"\"\n        Check if T can be placed starting at index i.\n        In the reverse process, we can place T at i if:\n        1. It hasn't been processed yet.\n        2. Every character S[i+j] either matches T[j] or is already 'cleared' (wildcard).\n        \"\"\"\n        for j in range(m):\n            if not covered[i + j]:\n                if s[i + j] != t[j]:\n                    return False\n        return True\n\n    # Initial check: find all indices where T matches S exactly\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if s[i:i+m] == t:\n            visited[i] = True\n            queue.append(i)\n            \n    # Process the queue: similar to a BFS\n    while queue:\n        curr = queue.popleft()\n        \n        # Mark characters as covered\n        for j in range(curr, curr + m):\n            covered[j] = True\n            \n        # Check neighbors: placing T at index 'curr' might enable placing T at \n        # overlapping positions (from curr - m + 1 to curr + m - 1)\n        start_range = max(0, curr - m + 1)\n        end_range = min(n - m, curr + m - 1)\n        \n        for i in range(start_range, end_range + 1):\n            if not visited[i]:\n                if can_place(i):\n                    visited[i] = True\n                    queue.append(i)\n                    \n    # If all characters in S are covered, it's possible to reconstruct S\n    if all(covered):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
