      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read L and R from standard input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    L = int(input_data[0])\n    R = int(input_data[1])\n\n    left_segments = []\n    right_segments = []\n\n    # Greedy approach from the left:\n    # Always take the largest possible power of 2 that divides L\n    # such that L + 2^i <= R.\n    curr_l = L\n    while curr_l < R:\n        # Find the largest i such that 2^i divides curr_l.\n        # If curr_l is 0, we can treat it as divisible by any power.\n        if curr_l == 0:\n            i = 62 # Sufficiently large\n        else:\n            # Equivalent to finding the number of trailing zeros\n            i = (curr_l & -curr_l).bit_length() - 1\n        \n        # We must also ensure 2^i fits within the range [curr_l, R]\n        while i >= 0 and curr_l + (1 << i) > R:\n            i -= 1\n        \n        if i < 0:\n            break\n            \n        next_l = curr_l + (1 << i)\n        left_segments.append((curr_l, next_l))\n        curr_l = next_l\n\n    # If the left-to-right greedy didn't reach R, it means we can refine it.\n    # However, for a single optimal path, we merge two greedy approaches:\n    # 1. From L, increase power of 2 as much as possible.\n    # 2. From R, decrease power of 2 as much as possible.\n    \n    # Resetting logic for a cleaner greedy:\n    res = []\n    l, r = L, R\n    \n    # Phase 1: From L upwards\n    while l < r:\n        # Find largest power of 2 i such that 2^i divides l and l + 2^i <= r\n        if l == 0:\n            # Special case for 0: find largest 2^i <= r\n            i = r.bit_length() - 1\n        else:\n            i = (l & -l).bit_length() - 1\n            \n        while i >= 0 and l + (1 << i) > r:\n            i -= 1\n        \n        # If we can't find a power that increases l without overshooting R \n        # while keeping the property 2^i * j, we check if the current 2^i is\n        # the best we can do for the \"upward\" phase.\n        # Actually, the simplest optimal construction:\n        # Find largest i such that l is multiple of 2^i and l + 2^i <= r.\n        # This builds the first half of the sequence.\n        \n        # But we must stop when we reach the \"peak\" (the largest power of 2 span)\n        # So we check if increasing further is possible.\n        if l + (1 << i) <= r:\n            # Check if this is the largest power we can use in the whole range [L, R]\n            # A segment [2^i*j, 2^i*(j+1)] is good.\n            # If we can use a larger i later, we stop this phase.\n            # This logic is handled by calculating from both ends.\n            break\n        l += (1 << i)\n    \n    # Correct Optimal Logic:\n    # We want to find the largest power of 2 segment [2^i * j, 2^i * (j+1)] \n    # contained in [L, R]. There might be multiple. \n    # The unique division is:\n    ans = []\n    cur = L\n    while cur < R:\n        # Largest i such that cur % 2^i == 0\n        if cur == 0: i = 60\n        else: i = (cur & -cur).bit_length() - 1\n        \n        # Ensure cur + 2^i <= R\n        while cur + (1 << i) > R:\n            i -= 1\n        \n        ans.append((cur, cur + (1 << i)))\n        cur += (1 << i)\n\n    print(len(ans))\n    for segment in ans:\n        print(f\"{segment[0]} {segment[1]}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
