    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_485",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums of length n, and a positive integer k.\nThe power of a subsequence is defined as the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence.\nReturn the sum of powers of all subsequences of nums which have length equal to k.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThere are 4 subsequences in nums which have length 3: [1,2,3], [1,3,4], [1,2,4], and [2,3,4]. The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,2], k = 2\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nThe only subsequence in nums which has length 2 is [2,2]. The sum of powers is |2 - 2| = 0.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,-1], k = 2\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nThere are 3 subsequences in nums which have length 2: [4,3], [4,-1], and [3,-1]. The sum of powers is |4 - 3| + |4 - (-1)| + |3 - (-1)| = 10.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= n == nums.length <= 50\n-10^8 <= nums[i] <= 10^8 \n2 <= k <= n",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Precompute combinations\n    C = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        C[i][0] = 1\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            C[i][j] = (C[i-1][j-1] + C[i-1][j]) % MOD\n\n    def count_with_min_diff_at_least(d):\n        \"\"\"\n        Counts subsequences of length k where the minimum absolute difference\n        between any two elements is at least d.\n        Uses DP: dp[i][j] is the number of subsequences of length j \n        ending at index i with min diff >= d.\n        \"\"\"\n        if d == 0:\n            return C[n][k]\n        \n        # dp[i][j] = number of subsequences of length j ending at index i\n        dp = [[0] * (k + 1) for _ in range(n)]\n        # prefix_sum[i][j] = sum(dp[0...i][j])\n        prefix_sum = [[0] * (k + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n        \n        left = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            # Maintain 'left' such that nums[i] - nums[left] >= d\n            while left < i and nums[i] - nums[left] >= d:\n                left += 1\n            \n            # Base case: subsequence of length 1\n            dp[i][1] = 1\n            \n            # For lengths 2 to k\n            for j in range(2, k + 1):\n                # We can append nums[i] to any subsequence of length j-1 \n                # ending at index p where nums[i] - nums[p] >= d.\n                # These are indices 0 to left-1.\n                dp[i][j] = prefix_sum[left][j-1]\n            \n            # Update prefix sums for the next i\n            for j in range(1, k + 1):\n                prefix_sum[i+1][j] = (prefix_sum[i][j] + dp[i][j]) % MOD\n                \n        return prefix_sum[n][k]\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    # The maximum possible min-diff is (max - min) / (k - 1)\n    max_d = (nums[-1] - nums[0]) // (k - 1)\n    \n    # We want Sum_{S} min_diff(S).\n    # This is equivalent to Sum_{d=1 to max_d} (count of subsequences where min_diff >= d).\n    for d in range(1, max_d + 1):\n        count = count_with_min_diff_at_least(d)\n        if count == 0:\n            break\n        total_sum = (total_sum + count) % MOD\n        \n    return total_sum\n```",
