    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThe AtCoder Kingdom holds a festival for N days. On M of these days, namely on the A_1-th, A_2-th, \\dots, A_M-th days, fireworks will be launched. It is guaranteed that fireworks will be launched on the last day of the festival. (In other words, A_M=N is guaranteed.)\nFor each i=1,2,\\dots,N, solve the following problem.\n\n- How many days later from the i-th day will fireworks be launched for the first time on or after the i-th day? If fireworks are launched on the i-th day, it is considered to be 0 days later.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nA_1 A_2 \\dots A_M\n\nOutput\n\nPrint N lines.\nThe i-th line (1 \\le i \\le N) should contain an integer representing the number of days from the i-th day until fireworks are launched for the first time on or after the i-th day.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\le M \\le N \\le 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\le A_1 < A_2 < \\dots < A_M = N\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 2\n2 3\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\n0\n0\n\nThe kingdom holds a festival for 3 days, and fireworks are launched on the 2-nd and 3-rd days.\n\n- From the 1-st day, the first time fireworks are launched is the 2-nd day of the festival, which is 1 day later.\n- From the 2-nd day, the first time fireworks are launched is the 2-nd day of the festival, which is 0 days later.\n- From the 3-rd day, the first time fireworks are launched is the 3-rd day of the festival, which is 0 days later.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n8 5\n1 3 4 7 8\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\n1\n0\n0\n2\n1\n0\n0",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input data at once for efficiency\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    M = int(input_data[1])\n    # A contains the days (1-indexed) when fireworks are launched\n    A = list(map(int, input_data[2:]))\n    \n    # Initialize an output list for all N days\n    # We will iterate backwards from day N to day 1\n    results = [0] * N\n    \n    # Use a pointer or a simple index to track the next firework day\n    # Since A_M = N is guaranteed, we can start from the last day\n    current_firework_idx = M - 1\n    \n    # Iterate from day N down to 1\n    for day in range(N, 0, -1):\n        # If the current day is past the firework day we are looking at,\n        # move the firework index to the previous one (though with reverse iteration, \n        # we check if the current day matches the firework day)\n        if current_firework_idx > 0 and day < A[current_firework_idx-1]:\n            # This shouldn't happen with correct logic, but helps visualize\n            pass\n            \n        # Update current_firework_idx if we passed a firework day moving backwards\n        # However, it's simpler to just check if 'day' is equal to A[current_firework_idx]\n        # or if it has passed it.\n        if day > A[current_firework_idx]:\n            # This shouldn't happen iterating backwards from N\n            pass\n        \n        # If the day is exactly a firework day, distance is 0\n        if day == A[current_firework_idx]:\n            results[day-1] = 0\n            # If there's a firework day before this, we might need it for previous days\n            if current_firework_idx > 0:\n                # We stay on this firework day until we go below it\n                pass\n        else:\n            # If we are before the current tracked firework day\n            if day < A[current_firework_idx]:\n                results[day-1] = A[current_firework_idx] - day\n            \n            # If day becomes smaller than the previous firework day, update index\n            if current_firework_idx > 0 and day <= A[current_firework_idx - 1]:\n                current_firework_idx -= 1\n                results[day-1] = A[current_firework_idx] - day\n\n    # Print all results separated by newlines\n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(map(str, results)) + '\\n')\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
