    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef minimumSeconds(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    To equalize the array to a specific value X, we need to find the maximum gap \n    between occurrences of X in the circular array. \n    If the maximum distance between two adjacent occurrences of X is 'd', \n    it takes floor(d / 2) seconds for X to spread to all elements in that gap.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    pos_map = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    # Store all indices for each unique number\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        pos_map[num].append(i)\n        \n    min_seconds = n\n    \n    for num in pos_map:\n        indices = pos_map[num]\n        # To handle the circular property, consider the gap between the last \n        # and the first occurrence by adding (indices[0] + n)\n        indices.append(indices[0] + n)\n        \n        max_gap = 0\n        for i in range(len(indices) - 1):\n            # The number of elements between indices[i] and indices[i+1]\n            # is (indices[i+1] - indices[i])\n            gap = indices[i+1] - indices[i]\n            if gap > max_gap:\n                max_gap = gap\n        \n        # The time needed for this specific number to cover its largest gap\n        # is gap // 2 because it spreads from both sides of the gap.\n        min_seconds = min(min_seconds, max_gap // 2)\n        \n    return min_seconds\n```",
