    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfWays(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    To split the array into good subarrays (each containing exactly one 1), \n    we must consider the number of zeros between consecutive ones.\n    If there are k zeros between two ones, there are (k + 1) possible locations \n    to place a cut. The total number of ways is the product of these possibilities.\n    \"\"\"\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Store the indices of all elements with value 1\n    ones_indices = [i for i, x in enumerate(nums) if x == 1]\n    \n    # If there are no ones, there is no way to split the array into good subarrays\n    if not ones_indices:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Start with 1 way (the base case for multiplication)\n    ans = 1\n    \n    # Iterate through consecutive pairs of indices of ones\n    for i in range(len(ones_indices) - 1):\n        # The number of zeros between two ones at indices i and i+1 \n        # is (ones_indices[i+1] - ones_indices[i] - 1).\n        # The number of ways to cut between these two ones is (zeros + 1),\n        # which simplifies to the difference of their indices.\n        gap = ones_indices[i + 1] - ones_indices[i]\n        ans = (ans * gap) % MOD\n        \n    return ans\n```",
