    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is true of mutation-based fuzzing?\n\nOptions: \nA. It only works for network-based fuzzing, not file-based fuzzing\nB. It modifies the target program's code to test its robustness\nC. It requires an initial error-free program to start the process\nD. It uses a random number generator to create new inputs\nE. It uses a fixed set of inputs to test the program\nF. It works by making small mutations to the target program to induce faults\nG. Each input is mutation that follows a given grammar\nH. It generates each different input by modifying a prior input\nI. It only makes sense for file-based fuzzing, not network-based fuzzing\nJ. It creates a completely new input for each test\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **H**.\n\nMutation-based fuzzing (also known as \"dumb\" fuzzing) starts with a seed input and generates new test cases by applying modifications such as bit-flipping, byte-shuffling, or arithmetic changes to that existing input. Unlike generation-based fuzzing, it does not require a formal grammar or knowledge of the input structure. While it often utilizes random number generators to decide which mutations to apply, its defining characteristic is that it evolves or modifies prior inputs rather than creating them from scratch.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
