    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9090",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Roughly how many bits are required on the average to describe to 3 digit accuracy the decay time (in years) of a radium atom if the half-life of radium is 80 years? Note that half-life is the median of the distribution.\n\nOptions: \nA. 50\nB. 35\nC. 10\nD. 40\nE. 28\nF. 16\nG. 24\nH. 45\nI. 19\nJ. 30\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the average number of bits (entropy) required to describe the decay time $T$ to 3-digit accuracy, we use the formula for the differential entropy of an exponential distribution. The decay constant $\\lambda$ is $\\ln(2)/t_{1/2} = \\ln(2)/80$, and the entropy $H(T)$ is $1 - \\ln(\\lambda) \\approx 5.74$ nats, which is $\\approx 8.29$ bits. To achieve 3-digit accuracy (1 part in 1000), we must add $\\log_2(1000) \\approx 9.97$ bits, resulting in a total of approximately $18.26$ bits. Among the choices provided, 19 is the most accurate estimate.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
