    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the minimum sampling frequency satisfying the Nyquist criterion, we first identify the highest frequency component in the signal $f(t) = 3\\sin(200\\pi t) + 6\\sin(400\\pi t) + \\sin(500\\pi t)$. The frequencies are $\\omega_1 = 200\\pi$, $\\omega_2 = 400\\pi$, and $\\omega_3 = 500\\pi$ rad/s, which correspond to $f_1 = 100$ Hz, $f_2 = 200$ Hz, and $f_{max} = 250$ Hz. The Nyquist criterion requires a sampling frequency $f_s \\ge 2f_{max}$, which equals $2 \\times 250 = 500$ Hz (or $1000\\pi$ rad/s, but the question asks for the value in \"$\\pi$Hz\" units/context corresponding to $2 \\times f_{max}$ in numerical terms or simply the numerical frequency limit).\n\n\\boxed{E}",
