    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9137",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Given two Boolean random variables, A and B, where P(A) = 1/2, P(B) = 1/3, and P(A | ¬B) = 1/4, what is P(A | B)?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1/6\nB. 0\nC. 1/3\nD. 1\nE. 1/2\nF. 5/6\nG. 1/4\nH. 3/4\nI. 2/3\nJ. 4/5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find $P(A|B)$, we use the law of total probability: $P(A) = P(A|B)P(B) + P(A|\\neg B)P(\\neg B)$. Given $P(A) = 1/2$, $P(B) = 1/3$, $P(\\neg B) = 2/3$, and $P(A|\\neg B) = 1/4$, the equation becomes $1/2 = P(A|B)(1/3) + (1/4)(2/3)$. Solving for $P(A|B)$ gives $1/2 = P(A|B)/3 + 1/6$, which simplifies to $1/3 = P(A|B)/3$, resulting in $P(A|B) = 1$.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
