  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9175",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many labeled graphs with a score of (6, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2) are there?\n\nOptions: \nA. 35\nB. 20\nC. 25\nD. 18\nE. 30\nF. 24\nG. 40\nH. 12\nI. 15\nJ. 10\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The \"score\" of a graph refers to its degree sequence. We are looking for the number of labeled graphs with 7 vertices ($v_1, v_2, \\dots, v_7$) having degrees $(6, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2)$.\n\n1. A vertex with degree 6 in a graph of 7 vertices must be connected to every other vertex in the graph. Let $v_1$ be the vertex with degree 6; there are $\\binom{7}{1} = 7$ ways to choose which labeled vertex has degree 6, but the problem implies the sequence is ordered or the set of degrees is fixed to specific labels. However, looking at the options and the nature of the problem, we assume $v_1$ is the specific vertex with degree 6.\n2. Once $v_1$ is connected to all other vertices ($v_2$ through $v_7$), each of those 6 vertices already has a degree of 1. To reach a degree of 2, each must gain exactly one more edge.\n3. The remaining edges must form a 1-factor (a perfect matching) among the remaining 6 vertices. The number of ways to partition 6 labeled objects into 3 disjoint pairs is given by $\\frac{6!}{2^3 \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{720}{8 \\cdot 6} = 15$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
