    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To produce a Gaussian matrix $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we use the transformation $\\mu + \\sigma \\cdot \\text{randn}$. Since $\\sigma^2 = 16$, the standard deviation $\\sigma$ is $4$, leading to `5 + torch.randn(10,5) * 4`. For the uniform distribution $U[-1, 1)$, the standard transformation from $U[0, 1)$ is `(high - low) * rand + low`, which results in `2 * torch.rand(10,10) - 1` (alternatively, the scaling `(rand - 0.5) / 0.5` is mathematically equivalent). Comparing the options, Option B correctly matches the Gaussian parameters and a standard PyTorch syntax for the uniform distribution.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
