      "generated_answer": "To find the maximum number of edges in a simple triangle-free planar graph, we use the specific form of Euler's formula for such graphs: $e \\le 2n - 4$. \n\n1. For a planar graph with $n$ vertices and $e$ edges, if it is triangle-free, every face must have at least 4 edges (since the shortest possible cycle is 4).\n2. Applying the handshake lemma for faces ($2e = \\sum \\text{deg}(f) \\ge 4f$) to Euler's formula ($n - e + f = 2$) yields $e \\le 2n - 4$.\n3. Substituting $n = 30$: $e \\le 2(30) - 4 = 60 - 4 = 56$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
