    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a CSMA/CD network with a data rate of 10 Mbps, the minimum frame size is found to be 512 bits for the correct operation of the collision detection process. What should be the minimum frame size (in bits) if we increase the data rate to 1 Gbps?\n\nOptions: \nA. 25600\nB. 10000\nC. 102400\nD. 32000\nE. 51200\nF. 6400\nG. 204800\nH. 256000\nI. 409600\nJ. 12800\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a CSMA/CD network, the minimum frame size is directly proportional to the data rate to ensure the transmission time remains at least twice the propagation delay ($L_{min} = 2 \\times \\text{distance} / \\text{propagation speed} \\times \\text{bandwidth}$). When the data rate increases from 10 Mbps to 1 Gbps (a factor of 100), the minimum frame size must also increase by a factor of 100 to maintain the same network diameter. Therefore, the new minimum frame size is $512 \\text{ bits} \\times 100 = 51200 \\text{ bits}$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
