      "generated_answer": "In cryptography, a Message Authentication Code (MAC) is considered secure only if it is computationally infeasible for an attacker to forge a valid tag. If the tag length is only 5 bits, there are only $2^5 = 32$ possible tag values, allowing an attacker to successfully guess the correct tag for any message with a high probability of $1/32$. Therefore, regardless of the key length or algorithm complexity, the MAC is insecure due to its susceptibility to existential forgery via brute-force guessing.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
