    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose Unix did not provide a way of passing file descriptors between processes, but still allowed inheriting file descriptors from a parent on fork and exec. What aspects of the OKWS design would break without file descriptor passing?\n1. It would be impossible for services to get a TCP connection to the client web browser.\n2. It would be impossible for okd to run as a non-root user.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, False\nB. Partially false, True\nC. Partially false, False\nD. True, True\nE. Partially true, True\nF. True, Partially true\nG. Partially true, False\nH. False, True\nI. False, Partially true\nJ. False, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the architectural constraints of the OKWS (OK Web Server) design.\n\n1. **Statement 1 is False**: Services do not necessarily need file descriptor passing to get a connection; they could theoretically be spawned *after* a connection is accepted (via fork/exec inheritance), though this would be less efficient than the existing dynamic process pool.\n2. **Statement 2 is True**: In OKWS, the `okd` (launcher) process typically needs to bind to privileged ports (like 80 or 443), which requires root; it then passes these descriptors to unprivileged processes. Without the ability to pass descriptors to already-running unprivileged workers or to receive descriptors from a dedicated logger/chroot manager, the privilege separation model that allows `okd` to relinquish root while maintaining service functionality would break.\n\nThe most likely intended answer in the context of OKWS system design is:\n\n\\boxed{H}",
