      "generated_answer": "All three considerations are correct principles in operating system design:\nI. **True**: Larger pages mean fewer pages total for a given process size, resulting in fewer entries in the page table and reduced overhead.\nII. **True**: Smaller pages reduce internal fragmentation because the unused space in the last page of a process is smaller on average.\nIII. **True**: Disk access time is dominated by seek time and rotational latency rather than data transfer time; therefore, reading a larger page takes nearly the same time as a small one, making larger pages more efficient for I/O throughput.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
