    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following points would Bayesians and frequentists disagree on?\n\nOptions: \nA. The use of probabilistic modelling for regression.\nB. The use of posterior distributions in a probabilistic model.\nC. The use of maximum likelihood estimation in determining parameters.\nD. The use of hypothesis testing in statistical inference.\nE. The use of empirical Bayes methods in estimating parameters.\nF. The use of frequentist methods in estimating population parameters.\nG. The use of a non-Gaussian noise model in probabilistic regression.\nH. The use of marginal likelihood in model comparison.\nI. The use of prior distributions on the parameters in a probabilistic model.\nJ. The use of class priors in Gaussian Discriminant Analysis.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The fundamental philosophical divide between Bayesians and frequentists lies in the treatment of parameters; Bayesians treat parameters as random variables and assign them prior distributions, whereas frequentists treat parameters as fixed, unknown constants. While both groups use likelihoods and various estimation techniques, the inclusion of subjective or objective prior distributions on parameters is unique to the Bayesian framework. Therefore, the use of prior distributions on parameters is the primary point of disagreement.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
