    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9438",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which among the following prevents overfitting when we perform bagging?\n\nOptions: \nA. The use of all data without sampling\nB. The practice of validation performed on a random subset of classifiers trained\nC. The use of underfitting to counterbalance overfitting\nD. The use of weak classifiers\nE. The use of classification algorithms which are prone to overfitting\nF. The use of classification algorithms which are not prone to overfitting\nG. The use of sampling with replacement as the sampling technique\nH. The practice of validation performed on every classifier trained\nI. The use of strong classifiers\nJ. The use of sampling without replacement as the sampling technique\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating), overfitting is prevented primarily by introducing diversity among the individual models through **sampling with replacement** (bootstrapping). This technique ensures that each classifier is trained on a slightly different subset of the data, which reduces the overall variance of the ensemble when their predictions are averaged. By allowing data points to be repeated or omitted in different samples, the model becomes less sensitive to the noise in the original training set.\n\nThe correct letter choice is \\boxed{G}.",
