  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9240",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| PCA and Spectral Clustering (such as Andrew Ng’s) perform eigendecomposition on two different matrices. However, the size of these two matrices are the same. Statement 2| Since classification is a special case of regression, logistic regression is a special case of linear regression.\n\nOptions: \nA. Neither true nor false, Neither true nor false\nB. False, Neither true nor false\nC. True, False, Neither true nor false\nD. False, True\nE. True, Neither true nor false\nF. Neither true nor false, True\nG. True, True\nH. False, False\nI. True, False\nJ. Neither true nor false, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, let's analyze both statements:\n\n**Statement 1:** This is **False**. PCA typically performs eigendecomposition on the Covariance matrix (size $d \\times d$, where $d$ is the number of features), whereas Spectral Clustering performs it on the Graph Laplacian (size $n \\times n$, where $n$ is the number of data points). These are only the same size if the number of features equals the number of samples.\n\n**Statement 2:** This is **False**. While classification can sometimes be framed as a mapping problem, classification and regression are distinct categories of supervised learning; specifically, Logistic Regression is a classification algorithm that uses a sigmoid link function, which makes it a form of Generalized Linear Model (GLM), but it is not a \"special case\" of Linear Regression (which predicts continuous values).\n\nBoth statements are false.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
