  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9847",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: By “animal motion,” Hobbes means:\n\nOptions: \nA. instinctive behavior, such as nursing young.\nB. irrational behavior.\nC. involuntary operations such as heartbeat and breathing.\nD. the physical actions and reactions of animals.\nE. human behavior that is driven by basic needs.\nF. all forms of non-human animal behavior.\nG. the process by which animals move from one place to another.\nH. behavior that is driven by fear or survival instincts.\nI. all voluntary behavior.\nJ. the movement and behavior of animals in the wild.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In Thomas Hobbes's *Leviathan*, he distinguishes between two types of motion: **vital motion** (involuntary actions like breathing and the pulse) and **animal motion** (also called voluntary motion), which consists of actions such as speaking or moving the limbs directed by the imagination and will. Therefore, \"animal motion\" refers to behavior initiated by the mind.\n\nThe correct letter choice is \\boxed{I}.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 182,
