    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2053",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Heteroscedasticity in a scattergram suggests that:\n\nOptions: \nA. there is a restriction of range of scores on the predictor and/or the criterion.\nB. the variability (range) of scores on the criterion varies for different scores on the predictor.\nC. there is a statistically significant correlation between the predictor and criterion.\nD. the predictor and criterion have a negative correlation.\nE. there is a perfect correlation between the predictor and the criterion.\nF. the predictor and criterion are independent of each other.\nG. the relationship between the predictor and criterion cannot be described by a straight line.\nH. the variability (range) of scores on the predictor is constant for all scores on the criterion.\nI. the scores on the criterion are uniformly distributed across the range of the predictor.\nJ. the variability (range) of scores on the predictor varies for different scores on the criterion.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Heteroscedasticity refers to a situation in which the variance of the residuals (or the spread of scores on the criterion) is not constant across all levels of the predictor variable. In a scattergram, this is often visualized as a \"fan\" or \"cone\" shape where the vertical spread of data points changes at different points along the x-axis. Therefore, it indicates that the variability of the criterion scores differs depending on the value of the predictor.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
