  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1983",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The denominator term in the F-ratio is reduced in magnitude by:\n\nOptions: \nA. decreasing both within-group and between-group variability.\nB. increasing both within-group and between-group variability.\nC. increasing between-group variability only.\nD. increasing within-group variability.\nE. decreasing between-group variability only.\nF. increasing between-group variability and decreasing within-group variability.\nG. decreasing between-group variability and increasing within-group variability\nH. increasing between-group variability and decreasing between-group variability.\nI. decreasing within-group variability and increasing between-group variability.\nJ. decreasing within-group variability.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), the F-ratio is calculated as the Mean Square Between (between-group variability) divided by the Mean Square Within (within-group variability). The denominator of this ratio represents the within-group variability (error variance). Therefore, to reduce the magnitude of the denominator term, one must decrease the within-group variability.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 202,
        "output_tokens": 78,
        "total_tokens": 280
