    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_2350",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is the best example of constant error\n\nOptions: \nA. A subject consistently provides correct answers to every test item\nB. A psychometrician consistently underestimates IQ scores by 10 points\nC. A test item is answered correctly by every student in a given class\nD. A subject consistently provides wrong answers to every test item\nE. A test-retest reliability coefficient is high for several samples\nF. A subject inconsistently provides wrong answers to every test item\nG. A psychometrician inconsistently overestimates IQ scores\nH. A test item is answered incorrectly by every student in a given class\nI. A test-retest reliability coefficient is low for several samples\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A constant error (also known as systematic error) occurs when there is a consistent displacement in one direction, such as a measurement tool or examiner consistently overestimating or underestimating a value. In this case, the psychometrician’s 10-point bias represents a systematic deviation from the true score. Unlike random errors, constant errors do not affect reliability but do impact the validity of the measurement.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
