    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **J**.\n\nIn the auditory system, the perception of increased loudness (amplitude) is encoded by the nervous system through two primary mechanisms: an increase in the number of activated hair cells and neurons (recruitment) and an increase in the rate or frequency at which those neurons fire. This distinguishes loudness from pitch, which is primarily associated with the specific location of activated neurons (place theory) or the timing of firing (frequency theory).\n\n\\boxed{J}",
