    "global index": "MMLUPro_psychology_1900",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: After World War II there was much speculation about the personalities of those who had committed atrocities. What do the findings of Milgram's studies imply about the personalities of Nazi war criminals?\n\nOptions: \nA. Only those with a high level of education and indoctrination committed the atrocities\nB. Only sociopaths would obey such harmful orders\nC. The actions were a result of a widespread cultural acceptance of violence at the time\nD. People with a predisposition to violence committed the atrocities\nE. Atrocities were committed by those who misinterpreted orders due to language barriers\nF. The atrocities were committed independently of any orders, based on personal beliefs\nG. Ordinary people would comply with the orders of a legitimate authority even to the point of committing cruel and harmful actions\nH. Only those with aggressive personalities committed atrocities\nI. Individuals who committed atrocities did so only when under direct threat to their own lives\nJ. Only those with a history of criminal behavior were capable of committing the atrocities\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Milgram's famous obedience experiments demonstrated that the majority of participants were willing to administer what they believed were lethal electric shocks to a stranger simply because an authority figure instructed them to do so. These findings suggest that the capacity for destructive obedience is not limited to \"monsters\" or those with specific personality defects, but is a potential behavior in ordinary individuals under certain situational pressures. Therefore, the study implies that many Nazi war criminals may have been common people following orders from perceived legitimate authorities.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
