    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Briefly trace historical attitudes toward human development emphasizing the debate between biological determinism and environmental determinism.\n\nOptions: \nA. Both endogenous (biological) and environmental factors are recognized as essential in human development.\nB. Development is solely determined by biological factors.\nC. Environmental factors shape development until adolescence, after which biology takes over completely.\nD. Biological factors set the potential for development, which is then unchangeable by environmental influences.\nE. Human development is a random process, not influenced by either biological or environmental factors.\nF. Human development is exclusively the result of personal choices, independent of biology or environment.\nG. Human development is predetermined at birth, with no influence from life experiences.\nH. Human development is entirely influenced by the environment.\nI. Development is solely determined by social interactions, with no biological influence.\nJ. Children are innately evil and need to redeem themselves.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The historical progression of developmental psychology has moved from extreme positions of \"nature\" (biological determinism) and \"nurture\" (environmental determinism) toward an integrated perspective. Modern consensus recognizes that human development is an interactive process involving both genetic predispositions and environmental influences. Therefore, the most accurate description of the evolution of these attitudes is the recognition of both factors as essential.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
