    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the validity of the argument, we look for a counterexample where all premises are true and the conclusion is false.\n1. From the third premise $\\sim(K \\supset J)$, we know $K$ must be true and $J$ must be false.\n2. If $K$ is true, the second premise $K \\equiv \\sim H$ requires $H$ to be false.\n3. If $H$ is false and $J$ is false, the first premise $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$ simplifies to $F \\equiv (I \\cdot T)$, which requires $I$ to be false; in this case, the conclusion $I \\equiv J$ becomes $F \\equiv F$, which is true (not a counterexample).\n4. If we test option D ($K=T, I=F, H=F, J=F$), all premises are true but the conclusion is true, meaning it is not a counterexample. However, testing the premises for validity shows that no assignment of truth values allows for true premises and a false conclusion (if $I \\equiv J$ is false, then $I$ and $J$ have opposite values, which eventually creates a contradiction in the premises).\n\nThe argument is valid because any attempt to make the conclusion false while keeping the premises true results in a logical contradiction.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
