    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_3",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Construct a complete truth table for the following argument. Then, using the truth table, determine whether the argument is valid or invalid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\n~G ⊃ H\n~H / G\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when G and H are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when G is true and H is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when H is true and G is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the validity of the argument, we examine the truth values for the premises and the conclusion:\n1. Premise 1: $\\sim G \\supset H$\n2. Premise 2: $\\sim H$\n3. Conclusion: $G$\n\nLooking at the case where **$G$ is false** and **$H$ is true**:\n- Premise 1 ($\\sim G \\supset H$): $\\sim F \\supset T \\Rightarrow T \\supset T$, which is **True**.\n- Premise 2 ($\\sim H$): $\\sim T$, which is **False**.\nLooking at the case where **$G$ is false** and **$H$ is false**:\n- Premise 1 ($\\sim G \\supset H$): $\\sim F \\supset F \\Rightarrow T \\supset F$, which is **False**.\n\nBy testing all possible truth values, we find that whenever both premises ($\\sim G \\supset H$ and $\\sim H$) are true, the conclusion ($G$) must also be true (this occurs only when $G=T$ and $H=F$). Since there is no row where the premises are true and the conclusion is false, the argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
