    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_11",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Construct a complete truth table for the following pairs of propositions. Then, using the truth tables, determine whether the statements are logically equivalent or contradictory. If neither, determine whether they are consistent or inconsistent. Justify your answers.\n(~M ⊃ ~N) ∨ (O ≡ N) and (~M · N) · [(~O ∨ ~N) · (O ∨ N)]\n\nOptions: \nA. Logically equivalent\nB. Contradictory\nC. Neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but consistent\nD. Inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the relationship between the two propositions, we analyze their truth values based on the variables $M, N,$ and $O$:\n\n1.  **Proposition 1:** $(\\sim M \\supset \\sim N) \\lor (O \\equiv N)$. This is a disjunction. It is true in most cases, specifically whenever $(\\sim M \\supset \\sim N)$ is true (which is whenever $M$ is true or $N$ is false) or whenever $O$ and $N$ have the same truth value.\n2.  **Proposition 2:** $(\\sim M \\cdot N) \\cdot [(\\sim O \\lor \\sim N) \\cdot (O \\lor N)]$. This is a conjunction that requires $\\sim M$ and $N$ to be true, and also requires $O$ and $N$ to have opposite truth values (exclusive OR). For Proposition 2 to be true, $M$ must be False, $N$ must be True, and $O$ must be False.\n3.  **Comparison:** If we plug the conditions that make Proposition 2 true ($M=F, N=T, O=F$) into Proposition 1, the first part $(\\sim F \\supset \\sim T)$ is $(T \\supset F)$, which is False, and the second part $(F \\equiv T)$ is False; thus, Proposition 1 is False whenever Proposition 2 is True. Since Proposition 2 is only true when Proposition 1 is false, and Proposition 2 is false in all other cases (where Proposition 1 might be true), the two statements can never be true at the same time, making them inconsistent.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
