    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_342",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Lucy Johnson earns $26,300 a year and supports her disabled husband and three children. Her husband does some work at home for which he earns $3,200 during the year. The Johnsons have itemized deductions amounting to $3,100 for the year. What is their federal income tax?\n\nOptions: \nA. $5,228\nB. $0 (because they fall below the tax threshold)\nC. $2,650\nD. $7,150\nE. $6,300\nF. $4,380\nG. $3,400\nH. $22,650\nI. $3,950\nJ. $1,850\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we must evaluate the Johnsons' taxable income based on standard U.S. tax principles (specifically historical standard deductions and exemptions relevant to such textbook problems). \n\n1.  **Total Income:** $26,300 (Lucy) + $3,200 (Husband) = $29,500.\n2.  **Taxable Income Calculation:** Even with itemized deductions of $3,100, a family of five (two adults, three children) in almost any modern tax year context would have combined standard deductions and personal exemptions (historically around $3,000–$4,000 per person) that far exceed their total income of $29,500.\n3.  **Conclusion:** Because their total exemptions and deductions would exceed their gross income, their taxable income is $0, resulting in a federal income tax of $0.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
