    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_421",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Although corporate profit maximization is not directly prescribed by major normative theories to which normative theory is it most similar?\n\nOptions: \nA. ethics of care\nB. preference utilitarianism\nC. Egoism\nD. Deontology\nE. Relativism\nF. The Categorical Imperative\nG. Virtue ethics\nH. Contractarianism\nI. hedonistic utilitarianism\nJ. Consequentialism\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Corporate profit maximization is most similar to **Egoism**, as both prioritize the self-interest of a specific entity (the corporation or the individual) as the primary goal of action. While egoism focuses on individual well-being, profit maximization functions as a form of \"institutional egoism\" where the organization's benefit is the ultimate measure of success. Other theories, like utilitarianism or deontology, require considering a broader range of stakeholders or universal duties that may conflict with maximizing a single firm's profits.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
