    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6111",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An ARMA(p,q) (p, q are integers bigger than zero) model will have\n\nOptions: \nA. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after p lags\nB. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after p+q lags\nC. An acf and pacf that are both zero after q lags\nD. An acf and pacf that are both zero after p lags\nE. An acf that is zero after q lags and a pacf that is zero after p lags\nF. An acf and pacf that both decline geometrically\nG. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after q lags\nH. An acf that is zero after q lags and a pacf that declines geometrically\nI. An acf that is zero after p lags and a pacf that declines geometrically\nJ. An acf that is zero after p lags and a pacf that is zero after q lags\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In time series analysis, the properties of the Autocorrelation Function (ACF) and Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF) differ based on the model type. While an AR(p) model has a PACF that cuts off after $p$ lags and an MA(q) model has an ACF that cuts off after $q$ lags, a mixed ARMA(p, q) model exhibits geometric (or exponential) decay in both the ACF and the PACF. This is because the AR components affect the ACF and the MA components affect the PACF in a way that prevents them from cutting off at any finite lag.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
