    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Monetarists believe that V and Q are\n\nOptions: \nA. always increasing.\nB. not critical for understanding how money affects the economy.\nC. not related.\nD. always decreasing.\nE. variable.\nF. unpredictable.\nG. inversely proportional.\nH. stable.\nI. directly proportional.\nJ. constant.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Monetarists base their theories on the equation of exchange ($MV = PQ$) and typically assume that the velocity of money ($V$) is relatively predictable or stable and that real output ($Q$) is determined by supply-side factors in the long run. By treating these variables as stable or constant in the short to medium term, they argue that changes in the money supply ($M$) are the primary drivers of changes in the price level ($P$). Therefore, in the context of classical monetarist theory, these variables are often simplified as being stable or constant.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
