      "generated_answer": "In macroeconomics, the Long-Run Aggregate Supply (LRAS) curve is represented as a vertical line because, in the long run, an economy's potential output is determined by resources and technology rather than the price level. This reflects the principle of monetary neutrality, where changes in the price level do not affect the economy's real GDP when all prices and wages have adjusted. Therefore, the long-run AS curve is independent of inflation or price changes.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
