    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The context of this question refers to the profit-maximization rule for hiring labor in a competitive market, which dictates that a firm should hire additional units of labor as long as the Marginal Revenue Product (MRP) exceeds the wage rate. The firm stops hiring at the point where the wage rate (Price of labor) equals MRP, meaning that prior to the final unit, $W < MRP$, and for the subsequent unit, $W > MRP$. Therefore, the decision is based on the transition where the cost of the next unit of labor would exceed its marginal contribution to revenue.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
