    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If an economy recovers from a recession (like the American recession of 1974-75), is the recovery considered growth?\n\nOptions: \nA. Economic reformation\nB. Economic decline\nC. Post-recession inflation adjustment\nD. Economic growth\nE. Temporary economic boost\nF. Economic rebound\nG. Cyclical economic renewal\nH. Structural economic change\nI. Economic stability\nJ. Not economic growth\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the distinction between long-term increases in productive capacity and short-term recovery. In macroeconomics, returning to a previous level of output after a recession is categorized as a recovery or \"filling the gap\" rather than true economic growth, which refers to an increase in the economy's potential output over time. Therefore, the recovery itself is technically not classified as sustained economic growth.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
