    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Near-money refers to highly liquid assets that are not cash but can be easily and quickly converted into cash or demand deposits without a significant loss in value. These assets serve as a store of value and are closely monitored by central banks as part of the broader money supply (M2 or M3). Examples include savings accounts, money market funds, and short-term certificates of deposit.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
