    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5664",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If a series, y, follows a random walk with drift b, what is the optimal one-step ahead forecast of the change in y?\n\nOptions: \nA. The current value of y\nB. The sum of all changes in y over the in-sample period divided by the number of changes\nC. The average value of the change in y over the in-sample period\nD. The current value of y minus the drift b\nE. One\nF. Zero\nG. The median value of the change in y over the in-sample period\nH. The current value of y plus the drift b\nI. The maximum value of the change in y over the in-sample period\nJ. The minimum value of the change in y over the in-sample period\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A random walk with drift is defined as $y_t = y_{t-1} + b + \\epsilon_t$, where $\\epsilon_t$ is white noise. The change in $y$ is given by $\\Delta y_t = y_t - y_{t-1} = b + \\epsilon_t$, and since the expectation of white noise is zero, the optimal forecast for the change is the drift $b$. In empirical practice, the drift $b$ is estimated by the sample mean of the changes in $y$ over the observed period.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
