    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5814",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the most important disadvantage of the diagonal VECH approach to building multivariate GARCH models that is overcome by the BEKK formulation?\n\nOptions: \nA. The diagonal VECH model does not provide accurate forecasts\nB. The diagonal VECH model is hard to interpret intuitively\nC. The diagonal VECH model contains too many parameters\nD. The BEKK model does not have the flexibility of the diagonal VECH model\nE. The BEKK model does not ensure a positive-definite variance-covariance matrix\nF. The diagonal VECH model does not allow for multivariate time series analysis\nG. The diagonal VECH model is not suitable for large datasets\nH. The BEKK model reduces the dimensionality problem that arises when a number of series are modelled together.\nI. The BEKK model is more computationally intensive than the diagonal VECH model\nJ. The diagonal VECH model does not ensure a positive-definite variance-covariance matrix\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **J**.\n\nThe primary disadvantage of the diagonal VECH model is that it does not automatically guarantee that the estimated variance-covariance matrix is positive-definite. The BEKK formulation (named after Baba, Engle, Kraft, and Kroner) addresses this specific issue by using a quadratic form that ensures the matrix remains positive-definite by construction.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
