  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6327",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How is the federal payroll tax that supports the Social Security program a regressive tax?\n\nOptions: \nA. The tax is regressive because only self-employed individuals are required to pay it.\nB. The tax is regressive because all workers pay the same percentage of their income.\nC. The tax is regressive because higher incomes are taxed at a higher rate.\nD. The tax is regressive because it only applies to the first $10,000 of income.\nE. The tax is regressive because it exempts all investment income.\nF. The tax is regressive because it includes additional deductions for higher income earners.\nG. The tax is regressive because there is a dollar limit on the amount of income, subject to the tax.\nH. The tax is regressive because lower incomes are not taxed to provide relief to lower earners.\nI. The tax is regressive because it is only applied to non-wage forms of income.\nJ. The tax is regressive because it is a flat tax that takes a smaller proportion of income as the income rises.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **G**.\n\nThe Social Security payroll tax is considered regressive because it only applies to earnings up to a specific annual cap (the Social Security Wage Base), meaning income earned above that limit is not taxed. Consequently, individuals with very high incomes pay a smaller percentage of their total annual income toward the program compared to those earning below the cap.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
