  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5593",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What could the government do to most effectively avoid a free rider problem?\n\nOptions: \nA. Supply public goods using tax dollars\nB. Initiate a lottery system for the usage of public goods\nC. Enact stricter antitrust legislation\nD. Increase the prices of public goods\nE. Allow private companies to supply public goods\nF. Tax those creating negative externalities\nG. Ban the use of public goods\nH. Provide more complete information about the relevant goods\nI. Reduce the production of public goods\nJ. Encourage voluntary contributions for public goods\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nThe free rider problem occurs when individuals benefit from resources, goods, or services (public goods) without paying for them, which leads to under-provision in a free market. By using tax dollars to supply these goods, the government ensures that everyone who benefits contributes financially, thereby overcoming the incentive to \"free ride.\"\n\n\\boxed{A}",
