  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6100",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following fiscal policy combinations would be most likely to slowly increase real GDP without putting tremendous upward pressure on the price level?\n\nOptions: \nA. Increase government spending with no change in taxes.\nB. Increase government spending with a matching decrease in taxes.\nC. Increase taxes with a decrease in government spending.\nD. Decrease government spending with no change in taxes.\nE. Decrease taxes with no change in government spending.\nF. No change in taxes with a decrease in government spending.\nG. Decrease government spending with a matching increase in taxes.\nH. No change in government spending with an increase in taxes.\nI. Increase taxes with a matching increase in government spending.\nJ. Decrease taxes with a matching increase in government spending.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we must identify which policy is expansionary (to increase real GDP) but also the most restrained (to avoid \"tremendous upward pressure\" on prices).\n\n1.  **Expansionary effect:** Increasing government spending ($G$) increases aggregate demand directly, while decreasing taxes ($T$) increases it indirectly via consumption; both options A, E, and I are expansionary.\n2.  **The Balanced Budget Multiplier:** Option **I** describes a balanced budget increase, where $\\Delta G = \\Delta T$. Because the government spending multiplier is larger than the tax multiplier (since part of a tax cut is saved rather than spent), an equal increase in both results in a positive but smaller, more controlled increase in GDP compared to deficit spending.\n3.  **Inflation control:** Because the net stimulus of a balanced budget increase is smaller than a pure increase in $G$ or a pure decrease in $T$, it is the choice most likely to grow the economy \"slowly\" without overheating the price level.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 217,
        "output_tokens": 218,
        "total_tokens": 435
      },
      "provider": "google",
