    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6135",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Adam Smith could count on self-interest to spur businessmen to greater efficiency. Now, with the rise of large corpora-tions , can modern economists also count on self-interest to produce efficiency?\n\nOptions: \nA. Self-interest always leads to efficiency in business\nB. Self-interest is only relevant in small businesses, not in large corporations\nC. Modern economists don't consider self-interest in their analysis\nD. Yes, modern economists can count on self-interest to produce efficiency\nE. Self-interest among modern businessmen is replaced by corporate social responsibility\nF. Modern economists rely solely on government regulation to ensure efficiency\nG. No, modern economists cannot count on self-interest to produce efficiency\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question addresses a core debate in modern economics: whether the \"invisible hand\" of self-interest (as described by Adam Smith) still functions effectively within the complex structure of large corporations. In large organizations, the separation of ownership and management often leads to \"agency problems\" where the self-interest of managers may conflict with the efficiency of the firm, making self-interest a less reliable driver of overall economic efficiency than it was in Smith's era. Therefore, many economists argue that self-interest alone is no longer a guaranteed catalyst for efficiency in the modern corporate context.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
