    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6249",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If a rich country and a poor country trade with each other, without any restrictions, and the rich country gains $50 million, while the poor country only gains $5 million, is the rich country exploiting the poor country?\n\nOptions: \nA. Exploitation cannot be determined solely based on monetary gains.\nB. The concept of exploitation does not apply to international trade.\nC. The rich country is not exploiting the poor country.\nD. Both countries benefit equally in terms of percentage growth.\nE. The trade terms are fair, and no exploitation occurs.\nF. The poor country is benefiting more, considering the relative size of its economy.\nG. The poor country is exploiting the rich country.\nH. The gain is not significant enough to determine exploitation.\nI. Both countries are exploiting each other.\nJ. The rich country is exploiting the poor country.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In classical economic theory, specifically the principle of comparative advantage, trade is considered mutually beneficial if both parties see an absolute gain, regardless of whether those gains are symmetrical. Because the poor country increased its wealth by $5 million through voluntary exchange without restrictions, it is better off than it was before the trade. Therefore, the simple existence of unequal gains does not constitute exploitation in an economic sense.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
