  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1065",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mary, a wealthy St. Petersburg widow, executed her first and only will on May 15, 1990 and died on August 18, 1990. Her will provided that her estate be divided equally between her only child, Joan, and the Salvation Army of Largo. How will Mary's estate actually be distributed?\n\nOptions: \nA. 50% to Joan and the income from the remaining 50% to Joan for life, remainder to the Salvation Army, if Joan files a timely petition protesting the devise to the Salvation Army.\nB. The estate is divided equally between Joan and the Salvation Army, but Joan has the option to reject her share.\nC. 50% to Joan and the income from the remaining 50% to the Salvation Army for life, remainder to Joan.\nD. 100% to Joan if she files a timely petition requesting that the devise to the Salvation Army be avoided.\nE. The estate is held in trust with Joan as the beneficiary during her lifetime, then transferred to the Salvation Army upon her death.\nF. 50% to Joan and 50% to the Salvation Army.\nG. 100% to Joan.\nH. 100% to the Salvation Army.\nI. 75% to Joan and 25% to the Salvation Army.\nJ. 25% to Joan and 75% to the Salvation Army.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is determined by Florida law as it existed in 1990. At that time, Florida's \"mortmain\" statute (former Section 732.803) allowed a child to void a charitable devise if the will was executed within six months of the testator's death, but the Florida Supreme Court declared this statute unconstitutional in May 1991 (*Shriners Hospitals for Crippled Children v. Zrillic*), and it was formally repealed thereafter. Since the statute was effectively voided or not applicable to prevent valid testamentary intent in modern probate, and under standard probate law where no valid challenge exists, the will's terms are followed.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
