    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_813",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Was the use of armed force permitted prior to the United Nations Charter?\n\nOptions: \nA. Armed force was only permitted in self-defense\nB. Armed force was permitted with many restrictions\nC. Armed force was not regulated under international law prior to 1945\nD. Armed force was permitted but required approval from an international body\nE. Armed force was prohibited\nF. Armed force was permitted but required approval from the countries involved\nG. Armed force was permitted subject to few restrictions\nH. Armed force was permitted with no restrictions\nI. Armed force was permitted with the consent of all nations\nJ. Armed force was not permitted under any circumstances\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Prior to the adoption of the UN Charter in 1945 and the Kellogg-Briand Pact in 1928, international law generally viewed the right to wage war as an inherent attribute of state sovereignty. While some frameworks like the League of Nations Covenant attempted to impose procedural delays, armed force remained a legal instrument of state policy subject to very few substantive restrictions. Therefore, compared to the modern prohibition, the pre-Charter era was characterized by the general legality of war.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{G}.",
