    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Bill purchased a can of Sipep from the Ajax Minimart. After he finished drinking the Sipep, Bill noticed that the can contained dead insects stuck on the inside bottom of the can. In a strict product liability tort action against Ajax, Bill must prove, among other things, that:\n\nOptions: \nA. Ajax is a merchant selling Sipep.\nB. Ajax knew or should have known of the defective condition\nC. Ajax had prior notice of other similar problems with Sipep products.\nD. Ajax actually placed the dead insects into the can.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To succeed in a **strict product liability** action, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant is a commercial supplier (a \"merchant\") who routinely deals in the type of product involved. Unlike negligence, strict liability does not require proof of fault, knowledge, or prior notice (ruling out B and C), nor does it require that the specific seller caused the defect (ruling out D). Therefore, Bill must prove that Ajax is a merchant to establish the basis for a strict liability claim.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
