    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_945",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A woman inherited her father's farm when he died testate. Prior to her father's death, the woman had already taken over the operations on the father's farm due to his declining health. Recently, the woman had granted a coal company rights to strip-mine coal from underneath the farm. Their agreement stipulated that the coal company would pay the woman a per-ton royalty for the coal extracted. In addition, the coal company agreed to fill in the excavated area and replace top soil on the surface of the land. After the coal company commenced its strip-mining operations, the woman noticed that the company was not filling in the excavated area as previously agreed. However, because the company paid the woman all the coal royalties from the strip mining, she did not voice any objection regarding its failure to replace the top soil. Two years later, the coal company had now completed its strip-mining operation under its arrangement with the woman. The coal company's right to strip-mine coal from the woman's property would be an example of a (an)\n\nOptions: \nA. constructive trust.\nB. easement appurtenant.\nC. servient estate.\nD. voluntary waste.\nE. license.\nF. easement in gross.\nG. tenancy in common.\nH. profit-a-prendre.\nI. adverse possession.\nJ. easement by necessity.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **H**.\n\nA **profit-a-prendre** (often simply called a profit) is a nonpossessory interest in land that gives the holder the right to enter another's land and remove resources, such as minerals, coal, or timber. Unlike a simple easement which grants a right of use, a profit specifically allows for the extraction and removal of a portion of the soil or its products. In this case, the coal company’s right to strip-mine and remove coal in exchange for royalties is a classic example of a profit-a-prendre.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
