    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the modern interpretation of the Import-Export Clause of the U.S. Constitution, specifically the Supreme Court's decision in *Michelin Tire Corp. v. Wages* (1976). The Court ruled that a nondiscriminatory ad valorem property tax on imported goods that have come to rest in a state is constitutional and does not violate the Import-Export Clause. Since the tax is applied to goods stored in the warehouse regardless of whether they are still in their original crates or mingled, both the bikes and the tires are subject to the tax.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
