  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1056",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Carol Collector was a serious antique car buyer, and was always searching for Thunderbirds in good shape. She saw a newspaper ad offering a 1964 Thunderbird sports coupe for $25,000, The ad also gave a website address \"to view a picture of the car,\" which Carol visited, and which showed a T-Bird with a perfect body and interior. Carol paid the $25,000 and signed a sales contract which specified that the Thunderbird was \"used and sold as is.\" When Carol went to pick up the car, she learned it was a 1968 model, which was not at all rare and worth much less than the advertised 1964 model. If Carol brings suit against the seller, the likely outcome is for\n\nOptions: \nA. Carol, because the seller was not negligent in providing a picture of the car.\nB. Carol, because the \"as is\" disclosure was not totally effective.\nC. The seller, unless they can prove Carol had knowledge that the car was a 1968 model.\nD. The seller, because the car was misrepresented in the advertisement.\nE. The seller, because the buyer was aware the Thunderbird was sold \"as is.\"\nF. Carol, unless the seller can prove the 1968 model was of equal value to the 1964 model.\nG. The seller, unless they were negligent in not explicitly stating the picture was of a new car, not the actual car to be sold.\nH. The seller, if Carol can prove she relied on the picture when making her purchase.\nI. Carol, because the seller provided a picture of the car, regardless of its condition.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **B**.\n\nThe \"as is\" clause in a sales contract typically disclaims implied warranties regarding the condition or quality of the goods, but it does not protect a seller against a failure to deliver the specific item described (the \"basis of the bargain\"). Since the contract was for a 1964 Thunderbird and the seller delivered a 1968 model, there is a breach of the express description, which an \"as is\" clause generally cannot override. Therefore, Carol is likely to prevail because the disclosure does not immunize the seller from delivering the wrong model year.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
