      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the interpretation of the Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause and the fundamental right to vote. While several options touch on discrimination, the strongest legal argument in a constitutional context regarding the mechanics of voting (like write-in requirements) centers on the unreasonable burden placed on a specific class of voters—in this case, those with limited English proficiency—which effectively acts as a discriminatory barrier.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
