      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **B**. Under the Dormant Commerce Clause, a state law that does not overtly discriminate against out-of-state interests but regulates evenhandedly to effectuate a legitimate local public interest (safety) will be upheld unless the burden imposed on interstate commerce is clearly excessive in relation to the putative local benefits (*Pike v. Bruce Church, Inc.*). Since the law applies to all private casinos regardless of ownership origin, it is constitutional if the safety interest outweighs the burden on commerce.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
