      "generated_answer": "To determine the answer, we analyze the provided context regarding physical quantities.\n\nThe text states that a physical quantity $x$ is expressed as the combination of a magnitude $n$ and a unit $u$ ($x = nu$). It further explains that the same physical quantity can be represented equivalently in different unit systems as $x = n_1 u_1 = n_2 u_2$.\n\nThis mathematical relationship implies that if the unit ($u$) changes to a different size, the numerical magnitude ($n$) must also change to keep the overall physical quantity ($x$) the same. For example, if you measure a length in meters ($n_1 = 1$, $u_1 = \\text{meter}$) and then change the unit to centimeters ($u_2 = \\text{centimeter}$), the magnitude must change to 100 ($n_2 = 100$) to maintain equivalence ($1\\text{m} = 100\\text{cm}$).\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of a physical quantity does indeed change when the system of units changes.\n\nFinal Judgment: 1\n\\boxed{Yes}",
