    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the two points be $(x_1, y_1) = (16, 54)$ and $(x_2, y_2) = (20, 53).$ \nSince $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are quadratic polynomials that share the same values at $x_1$ and $x_2,$ their difference $D(x) = P(x) - Q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial with roots at $x_1$ and $x_2$ and leading coefficient $2 - (-2) = 4,$ so $P(x) - Q(x) = 4(x - 16)(x - 20).$\nTheir sum $S(x) = P(x) + Q(x)$ is a polynomial of degree at most 1 because the $x^2$ terms cancel out ($2x^2 + (-2x^2) = 0$), and since $S(16) = 54 + 54 = 108$ and $S(20) = 53 + 53 = 106,$ we can find the linear equation $S(x) = 108 + \\frac{106 - 108}{20 - 16}(x - 16) = 108 - \\frac{1}{2}(x - 16).$\nTo find $P(0) + Q(0),$ we evaluate $S(0) = 108 - \\frac{1}{2}(0 - 16) = 108 + 8 = 116.$\n\n\\boxed{116}",
