    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we first analyze the function $h(t) = 4g(f(t))$.\nSubstituting $f(t) = ||t| - \\frac{1}{2}|$ into $g(x) = ||x| - \\frac{1}{4}|$, we get $g(f(t)) = |||t| - \\frac{1}{2}| - \\frac{1}{4}|$. Multiplying by 4 gives $h(t) = |4||t| - \\frac{1}{2}| - 1| = ||4|t| - 2| - 1|$. For $t \\in [-1, 1]$, the graph of $h(t)$ is a $W$-shaped wave oscillating between 0 and 1, specifically completing 4 linear segments (up-down-up-down) with peaks at $t=0, \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\pm 1$ and roots at $t = \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\pm \\frac{3}{4}$.\n\nThe equations are $y = h(\\sin(2\\pi x))$ and $x = h(\\cos(3\\pi y))$.\n1. $y = h(\\sin(2\\pi x))$ defines $y$ as a periodic function of $x$ on $[0, 1]$. Since $\\sin(2\\pi x)$ covers $[-1, 1]$ twice, and $h(t)$ has 4 \"legs\" on $[-1, 1]$, $y(x)$ oscillates between 0 and 1 exactly 8 times over $x \\in [0, 1]$.\n2. Similarly, $x = h(\\cos(3\\pi y))$ defines $x$ as a periodic function of $y$. Since $\\cos(3\\pi y)$ covers $[-1, 1]$ three times, $x(y)$ oscillates between 0 and 1 exactly 12 times over $y \\in [0, 1]$.\n\nThe problem asks for the number of intersections of these two curves in the unit square $[0, 1] \\times [0, 1]$. The first curve consists of 16 monotonic segments crossing the square vertically, and the second curve consists of 24 monotonic segments crossing the square horizontally. Every segment of the first curve intersects every segment of the second curve exactly once because they span the full range $[0, 1]$ in their respective directions. Thus, the total number of intersections is $16 \\times 24 = 384$.\n\n\\boxed{384}",
