    "global index": "AIME_89",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is a collection of $25$ indistinguishable white chips and $25$ indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the $25$ unit cells of a $5\\times5$ grid such that: \n\neach cell contains at most one chip\nall chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour\nany additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we must satisfy three conditions: at most one chip per cell, uniform color in each row and column, and maximality (no more chips can be added).\n\nLet $R_w$ and $C_w$ be the sets of rows and columns containing white chips, and $R_b$ and $C_b$ be the sets of rows and columns containing black chips. For the colors to be consistent in every row and column, any cell $(i, j)$ where $i \\in R_w$ and $j \\in C_w$ must be white, and any cell $(i, j)$ where $i \\in R_b$ and $j \\in C_b$ must be black; furthermore, the sets of rows and columns must be disjoint ($R_w \\cap R_b = \\emptyset$ and $C_w \\cap C_b = \\emptyset$) to prevent color conflicts. For the configuration to be maximal, every row and every column must be assigned a color ($R_w \\cup R_b = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$ and $C_w \\cup C_b = \\{1, \\dots, 5\\}$), and every intersection cell must be filled.\n\nLet $k$ be the number of rows assigned to white chips ($|R_w| = k$, so $|R_b| = 5-k$) and $m$ be the number of columns assigned to white chips ($|C_w| = m$, so $|C_b| = 5-m$). The total number of ways to choose these sets is:\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^5 \\sum_{m=0}^5 \\binom{5}{k} \\binom{5}{m}$$\nHowever, if $k=0$ or $k=5$, or $m=0$ or $m=5$, one color might not be used, and its corresponding set of rows/columns would be empty, potentially leading to empty rows/cols that could theoretically take either color; but the maximality condition implies all 25 cells must be filled (white at intersections of $R_w, C_w$ and black at $R_b, C_b$). Since we have 25 chips of each color, any partition of rows and columns is valid because the maximum number of chips of one color used is $5 \\times 5 = 25$.\n\nUsing the binomial identity $\\sum_{i=0}^n \\binom{n}{i} = 2^n$, the total number of ways is:\n$$\\left( \\sum_{k=0}^5 \\binom{5}{k} \\right) \\times \\left( \\sum_{m=0}^5 \\binom{5}{m} \\right) = 2^5 \\times 2^5 = 32 \\times 32 = 1024$$\n\n\\boxed{1024}",
