    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the speed of the boat in still water be $x$ km/h and the speed of the stream be $y$ km/h. Let the upstream speed be $u = x - y$ and the downstream speed be $v = x + y$.\n\nBased on the given information, we can set up the following equations for time:\n1) $\\frac{30}{u} + \\frac{44}{v} = 10$\n2) $\\frac{40}{u} + \\frac{55}{v} = 13$\n\nLet $a = \\frac{1}{u}$ and $b = \\frac{1}{v}$. The equations become $30a + 44b = 10$ and $40a + 55b = 13$. Solving this system, we multiply the first by 4 and the second by 3 to get $120a + 176b = 40$ and $120a + 165b = 39$; subtracting them gives $11b = 1$, so $b = \\frac{1}{11}$ and $a = \\frac{1}{5}$.\n\nThus, $u = x - y = 5$ and $v = x + y = 11$. Adding these equations gives $2x = 16$, so $x = 8$, and subtracting gives $2y = 6$, so $y = 3$.\n\nSpeed of boat in still water: 8 km/h, Speed of stream: 3 km/h.\n\n\\boxed{8 \\text{ km/h and } 3 \\text{ km/h}}",
