  {
    "global index": "AsDiv_1433",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: A man walks certain distance with certain speed. If he walks 1/2 km/hr faster, he takes 1 hr less. But if he walks 1 km/hr slower, he takes 3 more hours.\n\nQuestion: Find the distance covered by the man and his original rate of walking.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the original speed be $v$ km/hr and the original time taken be $t$ hours. The distance is $d = v \\times t$.\n\nFrom the first condition, if the speed increases by 1/2 km/hr, the time decreases by 1 hour:\n$(v + 0.5)(t - 1) = vt \\implies vt - v + 0.5t - 0.5 = vt \\implies 0.5t - v = 0.5 \\implies t - 2v = 1$ (Equation 1)\n\nFrom the second condition, if the speed decreases by 1 km/hr, the time increases by 3 hours:\n$(v - 1)(t + 3) = vt \\implies vt + 3v - t - 3 = vt \\implies 3v - t = 3$ (Equation 2)\n\nAdding Equation 1 and Equation 2:\n$(t - 2v) + (3v - t) = 1 + 3 \\implies v = 4$\nSubstituting $v = 4$ into Equation 2: $3(4) - t = 3 \\implies 12 - t = 3 \\implies t = 9$.\nThe distance $d = v \\times t = 4 \\times 9 = 36$ km.\n\n\\boxed{36\\text{ km, } 4\\text{ km/hr}}",
      "success": true,
